    "proseHtml": "<p class=\"lead\">Brisbane has no shortage of freight companies, which is good for choice and confusing when you're trying to pick one. The cheapest quote isn't always the best value, and the biggest name isn't always the right fit for your freight. So how do you choose well?</p><p>This guide runs through the questions worth asking before you hand a transport company your goods, so you end up with a carrier you can rely on rather than one you're always chasing.</p><h2>Start with compliance</h2><p>Before anything else, check that a carrier takes safety and compliance seriously. Ask whether they operate under Chain of Responsibility and whether they're NHVR compliant. This isn't box-ticking. A carrier that cuts corners on load restraint or driver fatigue is a risk to your freight and, because of COR, potentially to your own business's liability.</p><p>A good sign is a company that can talk clearly about how they restrain loads and manage their vehicles. If compliance is an afterthought for them, keep looking.</p><h2>Look at the equipment</h2><p>The right trucks make the difference between a smooth delivery and a failed one. Two things are worth checking.</p><h2>Load-rated restraint</h2><p>Ask whether their trailers run load-rated restraint. Load-rated equipment secures freight to a certified standard rather than relying on whatever straps are handy, which means your goods travel safely and loads turn around faster.</p><h2>Range of vehicles</h2><p>A carrier with only one type of truck will try to make your freight fit it. One with utes, tail-lift trucks, tautliners, flat-tops and semis can match the right vehicle to each job. That range matters most for awkward deliveries, like a heavy item to a site with no forklift.</p><h2>Check the coverage</h2><p>Think about where your freight actually goes. If you deliver across South East Queensland and into Northern NSW, a carrier who covers that whole footprint themselves is far better than one who hands your freight to another company at the edge of their area. Every handover adds time, cost and a chance of damage.</p><p>One carrier across your whole delivery area also means one point of contact and one team accountable, rather than chasing a subcontractor you've never spoken to.</p><h2>Ask about reliability and flexibility</h2><p>Reliability is the whole job, but it's hard to see in a quote. A few questions help. Can they deliver after hours or early morning if your freight needs it? Can they handle urgent, same-day work? Can they cover your regular runs if your own driver is off sick?</p><p>A carrier who can flex around your real operating hours is worth far more than one who only runs nine to five, because freight rarely keeps to convenient timing.</p><h2>Weigh up value, not just price</h2><p>It's tempting to pick the lowest quote, but freight is one area where cheap can get expensive. A damaged pallet, a missed delivery window or a failed drop costs far more than the few dollars you saved on the rate. Look at the whole picture: compliance, equipment, coverage, reliability and how easy the company is to deal with.</p><p>Often the best value is a carrier who prices fairly, turns up reliably and handles your freight properly, so you're not paying for problems down the line.</p><h2>A quick checklist</h2><p>When you're comparing Brisbane freight companies, ask:</p><ul><li>Are you NHVR compliant and do you work to Chain of Responsibility?</li><li>Do your trailers run load-rated restraint?</li><li>What range of trucks do you have for different loads?</li><li>Do you cover my whole delivery area on your own vehicles?</li><li>Can you handle after-hours, same-day and relief cover?</li><li>How do you handle it if something goes wrong?</li></ul><h2>Why businesses choose Road Linx</h2><p>At Road Linx, we're built around exactly these things. We're NHVR compliant and work to Chain of Responsibility, our trailers run load-rated restraint, and our fleet covers utes through to 24-pallet semis. We carry your freight across Brisbane, South East Queensland and Northern NSW on our own trucks, and we run after-hours, same-day and relief work because we know freight doesn't keep office hours. If that's the kind of carrier you're after, we'd be glad to talk through your freight.</p><h2>Red flags to watch for</h2><p>Just as useful as knowing what to look for is knowing what to avoid. A few warning signs are worth heeding when you're comparing Brisbane carriers.</p><p>Be wary of a quote that's dramatically cheaper than everyone else's, because something is usually being cut to get there, whether that's compliance, insurance or reliability. Be cautious if a company is vague about how they restrain loads or won't confirm they're NHVR compliant. And take note if they're hard to reach before you've even booked, because that rarely improves once they have your freight.</p><p>The best carriers are straightforward. They quote fairly, answer your questions clearly, and are easy to get hold of. If dealing with a company feels like hard work at the quoting stage, it's a fair sign of what the service will be like when something goes wrong.</p><h2>The bottom line</h2><p>The right Brisbane freight company isn't the cheapest quote, it's the one that's compliant, well-equipped, covers your area on its own trucks, and turns up reliably. Ask the questions above, watch for the red flags, and judge on value across the whole job rather than the headline rate.</p>",
    "proseStyle": "max-width: 100%;",
