    "proseHtml": "<p class=\"lead\">If you send freight, the trailer it travels on matters more than you might think. The right trailer means faster loading, better protection and a safer load. The wrong one means delays, damage or freight that won't fit at all. This guide walks through the main trailer types and when to use each.</p><p>We'll cover the three you'll hear about most, tautliners, flat-tops and drop-decks, and clear up the tautliner versus curtainsider confusion along the way.</p><h2>Tautliners and curtainsiders</h2><p>Let's settle the naming first, because it trips people up. A tautliner and a curtainsider are the same thing. Both describe a trailer with tensioned curtain sides instead of solid walls. Tautliner is a term that came from a brand name and stuck, while curtainsider is the plain description. Use either, they mean the same trailer.</p><p>The curtains slide open along the whole side, so a forklift can load or unload any pallet directly from the side rather than reaching in from the back. Then they close and tension to keep weather off the freight. For most palletised stock, this combination of fast access and protection makes the tautliner the default choice.</p><h2>When to use a tautliner</h2><ul><li>Palletised stock of almost any kind</li><li>Cartons and packaged goods that need to stay dry</li><li>Freight where fast loading and unloading saves time and money</li><li>Mixed loads a forklift needs to reach across the deck</li></ul><h2>Flat-tops (flatbeds)</h2><p>A flat-top, also called a flatbed, is an open deck with no sides or roof. That openness is the whole point. Freight can be loaded by crane from above or forklift from any side, with nothing in the way.</p><p>Flat-tops are built for freight that a tautliner can't handle: steel, timber, machinery, oversize items and anything craned into place. The trade-off is that there's no weather protection and the load has to be restrained entirely with straps, chains and gates, so correct restraint is critical.</p><h2>When to use a flat-top</h2><ul><li>Steel, timber and long building materials</li><li>Machinery and plant that has to be craned on</li><li>Oversize or awkwardly shaped loads</li><li>Anything too big or heavy for curtains</li></ul><h2>Drop-decks</h2><p>A drop-deck is a flat-top with a lower rear section. That lower deck is there for one reason: height. Trucks have a legal height limit, and a tall load on a standard flat-top can breach it. Dropping the deck lower lets the same tall load travel legally.</p><p>If you've ever been told a load is too tall to move on a standard trailer, a drop-deck is usually the fix. Drop-deck tautliners exist too, giving you the extra height allowance with weather protection.</p><h2>How to choose</h2><p>The decision comes down to a few simple questions. Does the freight need weather protection? If yes, lean tautliner. Is it oversize, craneable or too tall or heavy for curtains? Then flat-top, or drop-deck if height is the issue. How is it being loaded and unloaded, from the side, the top, or by crane? That often decides it on its own.</p><p>A common mistake is forcing freight onto the wrong trailer because it's what's available. A tall load squeezed onto a standard flat-top becomes an over-height problem, and delicate palletised stock on an open deck arrives wet or damaged. Matching the trailer to the freight avoids both.</p><h2>The right trailer, every time</h2><p>At Road Linx we run tautliners, flat-tops and drop-decks, in 22 and 24-pallet configurations, so we can match the trailer to your load rather than making your load fit the trailer. If you're not sure which suits your freight, tell us what you're moving, the dimensions and how it loads, and we'll put it on the right deck.</p><h2>Restraint changes with the trailer</h2><p>One thing people miss is that the trailer affects how a load is restrained. On a load-rated tautliner, the curtains and internal gear form part of the restraint system, so a compliant curtainsider can often be closed up and moving quickly.</p><p>A flat-top has no walls or curtains doing any of the work, so everything relies on straps, chains and gates applied correctly to the freight. That's not a drawback, it's just a different job, and it's why open-deck loads take more care and why the driver's restraint skill matters more there.</p><p>When you're choosing a trailer, it's worth thinking about restraint as well as loading and protection. The easiest, fastest and safest option for most palletised freight is a load-rated tautliner, which is a big part of why they're so common on our roads.</p><h2>The bottom line</h2><p>Match the trailer to the freight and everything downstream gets easier. Tautliners for palletised stock that needs protection and fast loading, flat-tops for oversize and craneable loads, and drop-decks when height is the problem. When in doubt, describe the load to your carrier and let them put it on the right deck.</p>",
    "proseStyle": "max-width: 100%;",
